WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.598
Central Ideas and Details

00:00:02.978 --> 00:00:07.004
Subject plus claim. Not too narrow, broad or extreme.

00:00:08.204 --> 00:00:09.295
How to spot it

00:00:09.695 --> 00:00:12.874
The question says “main idea of the text”

00:00:13.424 --> 00:00:15.708
A short passage, sometimes a story

00:00:16.258 --> 00:00:19.402
It’s testing: the author’s central point

00:00:20.602 --> 00:00:21.368
The method

00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:24.238
Step 1: Find the subject

00:00:24.518 --> 00:00:26.004
Who or what is this about?

00:00:26.554 --> 00:00:28.269
Step 2: Find the claim

00:00:28.549 --> 00:00:31.934
What’s said about it? Watch “but” and “however”.

00:00:32.484 --> 00:00:35.526
Step 3: Predict in one sentence

00:00:35.806 --> 00:00:37.281
Subject + claim.

00:00:37.831 --> 00:00:40.513
Step 4: Filter the choices

00:00:40.793 --> 00:00:43.229
Cut too narrow, too broad, too extreme.

00:00:43.779 --> 00:00:44.873
In a few words

00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:47.778
Subject. Claim. Filter.

00:00:48.978 --> 00:00:50.218
Answer filters

00:00:50.618 --> 00:00:51.448
Too narrow

00:00:51.728 --> 00:00:53.947
One true detail, not the whole point.

00:00:54.497 --> 00:00:55.338
Too broad

00:00:55.618 --> 00:00:57.769
Bigger than what the passage covers.

00:00:58.319 --> 00:00:59.321
Too extreme

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:03.321
“Proves”, “always”, “never” on a careful passage.

00:01:03.871 --> 00:01:04.942
After the turn

00:01:05.222 --> 00:01:08.180
The point usually follows “but” or “however”.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.441
Let’s try one

00:01:10.841 --> 00:01:16.631
For much of the twentieth century, scientists assumed that plants were passive organisms that simply

00:01:16.631 --> 00:01:19.050
responded to sunlight and water.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:25.504
Recent studies, however, show that plants can detect chemical signals released by damaged neighbors

00:01:25.504 --> 00:01:29.335
and respond by producing defensive compounds of their own.

00:01:29.335 --> 00:01:35.229
Some species even appear to adjust their growth depending on whether nearby plants are relatives.

00:01:35.779 --> 00:01:38.972
Which choice best states the main idea of the text?

00:01:39.622 --> 00:01:43.045
First, the old view: scientists assumed plants were passive.

00:01:43.595 --> 00:01:49.586
Then the turn, however: plants can detect signals and respond. That’s the main idea.

00:01:50.136 --> 00:01:57.187
Too narrow: C, just one detail. Too extreme: D. Not the point: A. The answer is B.

00:01:58.387 --> 00:01:59.456
Common traps

00:01:59.856 --> 00:02:00.961
The true detail

00:02:01.241 --> 00:02:03.171
Accurate, but only one piece.

00:02:03.721 --> 00:02:04.714
Word matching

00:02:04.994 --> 00:02:06.716
Same words, different meaning.

00:02:07.916 --> 00:02:09.914
Central Ideas and Details

00:02:10.194 --> 00:02:12.949
Subject. Claim. Filter.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:14.079
Spot it

00:02:14.279 --> 00:02:16.136
“main idea of the text”

00:02:16.686 --> 00:02:17.392
Steps

00:02:17.592 --> 00:02:19.104
1. Find the subject

00:02:19.384 --> 00:02:22.052
2. Find the claim (watch but / however)

00:02:22.332 --> 00:02:25.087
3. Predict: subject + claim

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:27.588
4. Cut too narrow / broad / extreme

00:02:28.138 --> 00:02:28.862
Remember

00:02:29.062 --> 00:02:30.972
Too narrow = one detail

00:02:31.252 --> 00:02:33.829
Too broad = more than the text covers

00:02:34.109 --> 00:02:36.636
Too extreme = proves, always, never

00:02:36.916 --> 00:02:39.218
The point often comes after the turn

00:02:39.768 --> 00:02:40.209
Trap

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:42.884
A true detail that’s only one piece

00:02:43.634 --> 00:02:47.052
